@node Tools Reference
@chapter Tools Reference
@cindex tools reference

This chapter documents every built-in tool.  Tools are the only way the
agent can interact with the outside world.  The planner names a tool in
each plan step; the executor calls @code{tool.run(args)} and passes the
result to the memory store.

Every tool follows the same contract:

@example
@r{Input:}  args dict          (keys and types documented per tool)
@r{Output:} @{ "stdout": str, "stderr": str, "returncode": int @}
@end example

A @code{returncode} of @code{0} means success; anything else means failure.
The executor stops and returns a @code{step_error} on the first failure.

Each tool declares the arguments it requires (marked @emph{Required.}
below).  The planner checks every step against them before anything runs;
a plan that misses one is sent back to the model once, then reported as
invalid (@pxref{Invalid Plan}).  Extra arguments are ignored.

@menu
* file_write::          Create or overwrite a file.
* file_read::           Read a file's contents.
* command::             Execute a shell command.
* patch::               Apply a unified diff to a file.
* web_search::          Search the web via DuckDuckGo Lite.
* search_embeddings::   Find semantically similar stored entries.
@end menu


@node file_write
@section @code{file_write}
@cindex file_write tool

Creates or overwrites a file with the given content.  Parent directories
are created automatically with @code{os.makedirs(exist_ok=True)}.

@subsection Arguments

@table @code
@item path
@emph{Required.}  Path to the file to write (relative to the working
directory or absolute).  Missing parent directories are created; a bare
file name such as @file{hello.py} writes to the working directory.  The
path, symlinks resolved, must be inside the project and not in its
@file{.git/} or @file{.codebud/}; anything else is refused before the
step runs (@pxref{What Is Not Protected}).

@item content
The complete text to write (an empty file if omitted).  The file is written in one
shot; any existing content is replaced entirely.
@end table

@subsection Returns

On success:
@example
@{ "stdout": "Wrote N bytes to /resolved/path", "stderr": "", "returncode": 0 @}
@end example

On failure:
@example
@{ "stdout": "", "stderr": "FileWriteTool error: ...", "returncode": 1 @}
@end example

@subsection When to use

Use @code{file_write} when you need to create a new file or replace a
file's entire content.  Prefer @code{patch} when you only want to change
a few lines in an existing file --- @code{file_write} requires the planner
to reproduce the complete file contents, which wastes tokens and risks
losing existing code.

@subsection Example plan step

@example
@{
  "id": "step_0",
  "description": "create hello.py",
  "tool": "file_write",
  "args": @{
    "path": "hello.py",
    "content": "print('Hello, world')\n"
  @}
@}
@end example


@node file_read
@section @code{file_read}
@cindex file_read tool

Reads the content of a named file and returns it in @code{stdout}.

@subsection Arguments

@table @code
@item path
@emph{Required.}  Path to an existing file.  Must be a file, not a
directory.  It may be outside the project, but never one of your secret
files (@file{~/.ssh}, @file{~/.gnupg}, @file{.netrc}, @dots{}) or
Codebud's own data, configuration and keys, symlinks resolved first.
@end table

@subsection Returns

On success:
@example
@{ "stdout": "<file contents>", "stderr": "", "returncode": 0 @}
@end example

On failure:
@example
@{ "stdout": "", "stderr": "File not found: path", "returncode": 1 @}
@end example

@noindent
Other errors (a directory, permission denied, @dots{}) are reported as
@samp{FileReadTool error: @var{details}}.

@subsection When to use

Use @code{file_read} only when you know the exact name of a file you want
to read.  Do @emph{not} use it to list a directory --- the planner
disambiguates this in a critical rule: use @code{command} with
@code{cmd: "ls"} to list directory contents.

@subsection Example plan step

@example
@{
  "id": "step_0",
  "description": "read README.md",
  "tool": "file_read",
  "args": @{ "path": "README.md" @}
@}
@end example


@node command
@section @code{command}
@cindex command tool
@cindex sandbox, command execution

Executes a shell command inside the @code{Sandbox}.  This is the most
general-purpose tool and covers all shell operations that the other
specialised tools do not address: running test suites, querying git,
searching files with @command{grep}, checking running processes, and so on.

@subsection Arguments

@table @code
@item cmd
@emph{Required.}  A shell command string.  The sandbox splits it with
@code{shlex.split()} --- shell metacharacters (@code{&&}, @code{|},
@code{>}) are @emph{not} interpreted, and pipelines are not supported:
@command{sh} is not on the allow-list.

@item network
@emph{Optional.}  @code{true} if the command needs the internet
(@command{pip install}, @command{git fetch}).  Every other command runs
without network (@pxref{Isolated Steps}).  A network step is shown and
confirmed like a web search, runs unattended in @code{full-auto}, and is
refused at privacy level @code{locked}.
@end table

@subsection Returns

@example
@{
  "stdout": "<command stdout>",
  "stderr": "<command stderr>",
  "returncode": <int>
@}
@end example

@subsection Constraints

@itemize
@item
The command's words must begin with an entry in
@file{harmless_commands.json} or @file{powerful_commands.json} (whole words,
not string prefixes), and some arguments are always refused
(@code{find -exec}, @code{python -c}, @dots{}).  Anything else is rejected by
the @code{SafetyEngine} before the step runs (@pxref{The Safety Engine}).
In @code{edit} mode every @code{command} step is refused.

@item
Powerful commands (@code{python}, @code{pytest}, @code{cp}, @dots{}) are
confirmed before running in @code{auto} mode and in @command{codebud run}
unless @option{--yes} is given.

@item
Catastrophic commands --- powering off, erasing a disk, recursively
deleting @file{/}, a system directory or a home directory --- are refused
regardless of the policy files (@pxref{The Sandbox}).

@item
Commands time out after 10 seconds.  Long-running commands (compilation,
large test suites) may need to be broken into smaller steps or the timeout
increased by editing @file{src/codebud/sandbox.py}.
@end itemize

@subsection Example plan steps

@example
@r{# List files}
@{ "tool": "command", "args": @{ "cmd": "ls -la" @} @}

@r{# Run the test suite}
@{ "tool": "command", "args": @{ "cmd": "pytest -q" @} @}

@r{# Search for a function name}
@{ "tool": "command", "args": @{ "cmd": "grep -rn 'def run' src/codebud/" @} @}
@end example


@node patch
@section @code{patch}
@cindex patch tool
@cindex unified diff

Applies a unified diff to one file.  Use this instead of
@code{file_write} when only a few lines need to change --- the diff is
smaller than the full file, which saves tokens and is less likely to
introduce accidental changes.

@subsection Arguments

@table @code
@item patch
@emph{Required.}  A unified diff string as produced by @command{diff -u}
or @command{git diff}.  Must include @samp{---} and @samp{+++} header
lines that identify the target file, which must be inside the project
and not in its @file{.git/} or @file{.codebud/}, like @code{file_write}.
@end table

@subsection Returns

On success:
@example
@{ "stdout": "Patch applied to <file>", "stderr": "", "returncode": 0 @}
@end example

On failure:
@example
@{ "stdout": "", "stderr": "PatchTool error: ...", "returncode": 1 @}
@end example

@subsection Example plan step

@example
@{
  "id": "step_0",
  "description": "change greeting to Spanish",
  "tool": "patch",
  "args": @{
    "patch": "--- a/hello.py\n+++ b/hello.py\n@@ -1 +1 @@\n-print('Hello, world')\n+print('Hola, mundo')\n"
  @}
@}
@end example

@subsection How a patch is applied

Strictly, so a patch either does what it shows or nothing:

@itemize
@item Each hunk is found by its context and removed lines: at the line
its @samp{@@@@} header names if they match there, otherwise at the
nearest place they do (a model's line numbers are often off).  Lines the
diff does not mention are kept as they are.
@item If a hunk's lines are found nowhere, or at two places equally near
the stated line, the patch fails with a message and the file is left
unchanged; add context lines to make it unambiguous.
@item A hunk with no old lines inserts: @samp{-N,0} after line @var{N},
@samp{-0,0} at the top of the file (as GNU @command{diff} writes it).
@item @samp{--- /dev/null} creates a new file from added lines.
@item A missing newline at the end of the file stays missing.
@item One file per patch; a diff touching several files is refused.
@end itemize

@subsection Limitations

Binary files and renames are not supported; use a @code{command} step
with the system @command{patch} or @command{git apply} for those.


@node web_search
@section @code{web_search}
@cindex web_search tool
@cindex DuckDuckGo

Queries DuckDuckGo Lite and returns up to five results as a JSON array.

@strong{This tool is disabled by default.}  Set @env{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED=1}
before running Codebud to enable it.  When disabled, the tool's description
in the planner prompt is replaced with a notice so the planner knows not
to use it.

@subsection Arguments

@table @code
@item query
@emph{Required.}  A search query string.
@end table

@subsection Returns

On success:
@example
@{
  "stdout": "[
    @{\"title\": \"...\", \"url\": \"...\", \"snippet\": \"...\"@},
    ...
  ]",
  "stderr": "",
  "returncode": 0
@}
@end example

On failure or when disabled:
@example
@{ "stdout": "", "stderr": "Web search is disabled...", "returncode": 1 @}
@end example

@subsection Example plan step

@example
@{
  "id": "step_0",
  "description": "search for Python async patterns",
  "tool": "web_search",
  "args": @{ "query": "Python async await best practices 2024" @}
@}
@end example

@subsection Privacy note

Queries are sent to @code{https://lite.duckduckgo.com/lite/} over HTTPS.
This is the only network call Codebud makes that goes outside your machine
(apart from Ollama on a remote host, or the @code{openai} backend), so the
query is always shown before the step runs, and each search needs a
confirmation: in @code{ask} mode with every step --- and still after you
answer @samp{a} (all) to run the rest without asking --- in @code{auto}
mode and in @command{codebud run} like a powerful command (@option{--yes}
allows it unattended).  The same holds for a command step that declares
it needs the network.  In @code{full-auto} mode searches run unattended, as all
steps do there; the query is still printed.  A project's
@file{config.toml} cannot enable web search (@pxref{Configuration File}).


@node search_embeddings
@section @code{search_embeddings}
@cindex search_embeddings tool
@cindex semantic search
@cindex project search
@cindex embeddings

Searches the current project's index, built with @command{codebud index}
(@pxref{The index Subcommand}), and returns the @var{top_k} chunks of code
or documentation most relevant to the query, each with its file and line
range.  Three rankings are combined (reciprocal-rank fusion):

@itemize
@item @strong{meaning} --- similarity of the query's embedding vector to
each chunk's;
@item @strong{keywords} --- BM25 full-text matches, for exact identifiers
and error messages;
@item @strong{names} --- chunks whose function, class or section name is a
word of the query, weighted double.
@end itemize

@noindent
Chunks from test files count half unless the query is about tests, since
tests mention every name they test (@pxref{Project Search Evaluation}).

The project is the git work tree containing the current directory.  The
tool fails with an error saying what to do when the project has no index
(@samp{Create one with: codebud index}), when @code{numpy} is missing
(@samp{pip install 'codebud[rag]'}), when the index was built with another
embedding model (@samp{codebud index --rebuild}), or when the embedding
model is unreachable (@samp{ollama pull @dots{}}).  It never substitutes
made-up vectors.  (The test suite sets @env{CODEBUD_EMBED_STUB} to
@samp{1} to use a deterministic offline stub instead.)

@subsection Arguments

@table @code
@item query
@emph{Required.}  What to look for, in words or by name.

@item top_k
@emph{Optional.}  Number of results to return.  Default: @code{5}.
(@code{k} is accepted as an older name.)
@end table

@subsection Returns

On success, @code{stdout} is a JSON array, best match first:
@example
[
  @{
    "path": "src/codebud/core.py",
    "lines": "202-226",
    "symbol": "AgentCore.compact_history",
    "score": 0.0636,
    "text": "def compact_history(..."
  @},
  ...
]
@end example

@noindent
@code{score} is the fused rank score: only its order is meaningful.
Overlapping parts of one file are returned once.

@subsection Environment variables

@table @env
@item EMBED_MODEL
The Ollama embedding model.  Defaults to @code{all-minilm}
(@command{ollama pull all-minilm}); it does not follow
@env{OLLAMA_MODEL}, and must be the model the index was built with.
@item OLLAMA_BASE_URL
The Ollama server.  Defaults to @code{http://localhost:11434}.
@end table

@noindent
Earlier versions searched @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/embeddings/index.json},
which nothing ever filled; it is no longer read and can be deleted.  (An
@code{embed} tool that returned a vector without storing it was removed
too.)
